---
import Button from "@/components/button/Button.astro";
import ShineText from "@/components/shine-text/ShineText.astro";
import { getLocaleFromUrl } from "@/js/localeUtils";
import { getTranslatedData } from "@/js/translationUtils";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const currLocale = getLocaleFromUrl(Astro.url);
const siteData = getTranslatedData("siteData", currLocale);
---

<BaseLayout title={siteData.title} description={siteData.description} navStyle="default">
	<!-- Hero / Intro -->
	<section class="site-container pt-24 pb-8 md:pt-28 md:pb-8 overflow-x-clip" data-mos="fade-up"
          data-mos-delay=".1"
          data-mos-anchor="#syllabus-title">
		<div class="max-w-3xl">
			<h1 class="h1 text-center md:text-left"><ShineText>Syllabus</ShineText> for EconRunes </h1>
			<p class="description mt-4">
				Recommended Course Outline (used throughout the course + optional capstone)
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- Content -->
	<section class="site-container markdown-content pb-20 md:pb-24" data-mos="fade-up"
          data-mos-delay=".2"
          data-mos-anchor="#syllabus-content">
		<h2>What EconRunes Covers</h2>
		<p>
			EconRunes is a story-driven journey through market structures. Students make decisions, see outcomes instantly,
			and connect choices to core models and graphs.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Realm 1: Perfect Competition</strong> — <em>choose quantity</em> as a price-taker: costs, MR = MC where MR = price, SR profit/loss, LR entry/exit toward zero economic profit</li>
			<li><strong>Realm 2: Monopolistic Competition</strong> — <em>set your price</em> on a downward-sloping firm demand curve, MR = MC (not P = MC), and watch demand soften as rivals enter across rounds</li>
			<li><strong>Realm 3: Oligopoly</strong> — <em>choose quantity</em> in a Cournot duopoly: find your best response to a rival's known output using residual demand</li>
			<li><strong>Realm 4: Monopoly</strong> — <em>choose quantity</em> as the sole seller: market power, MR &lt; P, MR = MC, and persistent economic profit (P &gt; MC, no entry to erode it)</li>
			<li><strong>Capstone: Council of Runes</strong> — an 8-round synthesis gauntlet that cycles back through all four market structures</li>
		</ul>

		<hr />

		<h2><span class="text-primary-accent">Part 1</span> — Setup Week</h2>
		<p><strong>Goal:</strong> get every student comfortable with the game and encourage experimentation.</p>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Assign Realm 1</strong></li>
			<li><strong>Play one low-stakes practice round in Realm 1</strong></li>
			<li>
				<strong>Review Outcomes:</strong>
				<ul>
					<li>“What decision did you change?”</li>
					<li>“What happened to price/quantity/profit?”</li>
					<li>“Which curve or concept explains it?”</li>
				</ul>
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p><strong>Instructor tip:</strong> Grade this as completion only.</p>

		<h2><span class="text-primary-accent">Part 2</span> — The Repeatable Weekly Loop</h2>
		<p><strong>Goal:</strong> keep EconRunes aligned with what you’re teaching without adding prep burden.</p>
		<p>Each time you use EconRunes, follow the same 4 steps:</p>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Teach the concept</strong> (your normal lecture/readings)</li>
			<li><strong>Play the Game</strong> — students play the Realm tied to that concept</li>
			<li><strong>Debrief</strong> — 3 questions (use the same ones every time)</li>
			<li><strong>Quick Grade</strong> — pick one concept</li>
		</ol>

		<h3>The 3 debrief questions (reuse every time)</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>What choice did you make, and why?</li>
			<li>What changed in the outcome (price, quantity, profit), and what concept explains it?</li>
			<li>What would you do next round—and what do you predict will happen?</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>The 3 easy grading options</h3>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Option A: Exit Ticket</strong> (3 short answers)</li>
			<li><strong>Option B: Screenshot + 3 sentences</strong> (what it shows + why it happened)</li>
			<li><strong>Option C: Mini-quiz</strong> (5 questions)</li>
		</ul>

		<hr />

		<h2><span class="text-primary-accent">Part 3</span> — Recommended Roadmap</h2>
		<p>
			Use EconRunes when these topics appear in your course. You don’t need to match weeks perfectly—just follow the order.
		</p>

		<h3>Realm 1 — Perfect Competition (Costs + profit maximization)</h3>
		<p><strong>Run when you teach:</strong> production, costs, SR vs LR, profit maximization (MR = MC)</p>
		<h4>Steps</h4>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Teach:</strong> cost curves + MR=MC idea</li>
			<li><strong>Play:</strong> Realm 1 segment (price-taking decisions)</li>
			<li><strong>Debrief:</strong> connect SR profit/loss to cost structure</li>
			<li><strong>Quick grade:</strong> screenshot + explanation</li>
		</ol>

		<h3>Realm 2 — Monopolistic Competition (Demand + differentiation)</h3>
		<p><strong>Run when you teach:</strong> demand shifts, elasticity intuition, differentiation</p>
		<h4>Steps</h4>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Teach:</strong> downward-sloping firm demand + product differentiation</li>
			<li><strong>Play:</strong> Realm 2 segment (set your price; quantity is read off the demand curve)</li>
			<li><strong>Debrief:</strong> why MR = MC (not P = MC) sets the best price, and what entry does to demand over the rounds</li>
			<li><strong>Quick grade:</strong> exit ticket</li>
		</ol>

		<h3>Realm 3 — Oligopoly (Strategy + rivals)</h3>
		<p><strong>Run when you teach:</strong> interdependence, pricing/quantity competition, basic game theory</p>
		<h4>Steps</h4>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Teach:</strong> why rivals matter + best response to a rival's output</li>
			<li><strong>Play:</strong> Realm 3 segment (Cournot duopoly — the rival's output is shown each round)</li>
			<li><strong>Debrief:</strong> “Given the rival's known output, what's your best-response quantity — and how does residual demand change it?”</li>
			<li><strong>Quick grade:</strong> mini-quiz or short reflection</li>
		</ol>

		<h3>Realm 4 — Monopoly (Market power + persistent profit)</h3>
		<p><strong>Run when you teach:</strong> market power, MR &lt; P, MR = MC, why monopoly profit persists</p>
		<h4>Steps</h4>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Teach:</strong> monopoly profit rule (MR = MC, MR &lt; P) and why monopoly profit persists</li>
			<li><strong>Play:</strong> Realm 4 segment (choose quantity; price is read off the demand curve)</li>
			<li><strong>Debrief:</strong> relate the MR = MC choice to persistent profit (P &gt; MC, no entry); note how demand's height and slope move the optimum each round</li>
			<li><strong>Quick grade:</strong> screenshot + explanation</li>
		</ol>

		<hr />

		<h2><ShineText>Part 4</ShineText> — Optional Capstone Event</h2>
		<p><strong>Goal:</strong> create a memorable finish with teamwork and synthesis—without adding complexity.</p>

		<h3>Capstone Steps (1–2 class sessions)</h3>
		<ol>
			<li><strong>Form teams</strong> (3–5 students)</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Team strategy plan:</strong>
				<ul>
					<li>Council cycles through all four structures — which decision rule applies in each phase (P = MC, MR = MC for price, residual MR = MC, monopoly MR = MC)?</li>
					<li>What’s our strategy for each phase and why?</li>
					<li>What do we predict for market conditions, and what will the results confirm or deny?</li>
				</ul>
			</li>
			<li><strong>Team run:</strong> play the Council of Runes (Realm 5) — 8 rounds spanning all four market structures</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Team deliverable (choose one):</strong>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>One-page memo</strong> (strategy → results → what changed → what you learned)</li>
					<li><strong>5-slide deck</strong> (same structure, more visual)</li>
				</ul>
			</li>
			<li><strong>Wrap-up discussion:</strong> compare strategies across teams</li>
		</ol>

		<h3>Capstone grading (simple rubric)</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>Completion + clarity of economic explanation (not “winning”)</li>
		</ul>

		<hr />

		<h2>Instructor Quick Start</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>Assign <strong>Realm 1 tutorial + practice</strong> in week 1–2.</li>
			<li>Repeat the loop all semester: <strong>Teach → Play → Debrief → Quick Grade</strong>.</li>
			<li>Run Realms in order as your course reaches each topic: <strong>1 → 2 → 3 → 4</strong>.</li>
			<li>If you want a finale, run the <strong>Capstone</strong> with teams and a one-page memo.</li>
		</ol>

		<div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3 not-content">
			<Button variant="primary" href="/subscribe/">Join the Waitlist</Button>
			<Button variant="secondary" href="/games/econrunes/" arrow="right">Explore EconRunes</Button>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>